{"obligations":[{"id":"ATTRIBUTE_COMBINATION","label":"V","source_lines":[83,90,91,161,162,163,164,165,167,168,169,170,171,173,174,175,176,178,179,181],"reason":"SPEC requires A to alternate producer/consumer episodes and B to run producer+consumer concurrently; but b_producer calls consumer_episode and b_consumer calls producer_episode, so both a_coro and b_coro invoke the same LEFT-arm producer/consumer functions. This does not implement the declared D3+LONG+LH attribute combination: B should run RIGHT-arm producer and consumer coroutines together, not duplicate LEFT-arm episodes."},{"id":"BUFFER_EVENT_PROTOCOL","label":"V","source_lines":[122,153,158],"reason":"ready is signaled after buffer departure (line 122) and cleared after carried move (line 153); empty_0 is published after target release and departure (line 158). However, the second-item producer waits and clears empty_0 (lines 96-99) before entering buffer, matching SPEC. The protocol order appears correct for the buffer mechanism, but the concurrent b_coro reuses the same shared ready_receipts/empty_receipt state and same event IDs, creating conflicting event consumption."},{"id":"BUFFER_OCCUPANCY_OWNER","label":"C","source_lines":[111,112,113,114,115,116,117,118,119,141,142,143,144,145,146,147,148,149,150],"reason":"Both producer and consumer acquire buffer_lock before buffer entry/departure and release it after departure. Producer acquires lock at line 112, moves to buffer, releases part, departs, then releases lock in finally (lines 118-119). Consumer acquires lock at line 142, moves to target, releases, departs, then releases lock (lines 149-150). Lock ownership spans the complete buffer contact period."},{"id":"BUFFER_RECEIPT","label":"C","source_lines":[122,123,134,135,145,153,154],"reason":"Producer signals ready with part (line 122) and stores receipt (line 123). Consumer waits the corresponding ready receipt (line 134), stores it (line 135), supplies that exact receipt on carried move to target (line 145), and clears ready after carried move using expected_version=r.version (line 153). This implements the BUFFER_RECEIPT mechanism correctly."},{"id":"CONTROL_FLOW_HOLDOUT","label":"U","source_lines":[83,161,165,167,168,171,173,176,178,179,181],"reason":"The holdout types are ATTRIBUTE_COMBINATION and LAYOUT_TOPOLOGY. The code's control flow for A alternates episodes (lines 161-165) and B gathers two coroutines (lines 178-179), but b_producer and b_consumer call the wrong episode types (consumer vs producer), making the actual control-flow signature ambiguous relative to the declared holdout."},{"id":"LONG_RANGE_DEPENDENCY","label":"C","source_lines":[90,91,93,95,96,97,98,99,101,102,103,104,105,108,109,111,112,114,115,117,122,124,125],"reason":"LONG requires acquiring tool before source pickup and keeping it across the second-item wait and checks. Line 91 acquires tool before source pickup. For i==1, lines 96-105 perform the empty_0 wait/clear and serial line_clear/receiver_ready checks while tool is still held. Tool is released only in the finally block (line 125) after the entire episode, spanning source, grasp, buffer placement, and empty receipt processing."},{"id":"SUPPORT_DEPARTURE","label":"C","source_lines":[107,108,109,111,112,114,115,116,117,137,138,139,141,142,145,146,147,148],"reason":"Every release is followed by an immediate same-arm separating move in the same coroutine. Producer releases part at buffer (line 115) then immediately moves to left_wait/left_home (line 117). Consumer releases part at target (line 146) then immediately moves to right_home (line 148). Approach-to-grasp sequences have zero intervening calls (lines 108-109, 138-139)."},{"id":"TASK_GOAL","label":"V","source_lines":[161,162,163,164,165,167,168,169,170,171,173,174,175,176,178,179,181],"reason":"The goal requires part_0 at target_0 and part_1 at target_1 with empty arms at homes. However, both a_coro and b_coro invoke the same producer_episode and consumer_episode functions that operate on LEFT (producer) and RIGHT (consumer) arms. Running these concurrently via gather (line 181) means the same episodes execute twice on the same arms with the same shared state, causing conflicting grasps, event receipts, and resource ownership rather than achieving the declared terminal state."}]}
